    "lawSection": "Mental Hygiene",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7753\n\nSPONSOR: BROUK\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to require the office of mental health and the department of\nhealth to conduct a study on the disparate impacts regarding postpartum\ndepression screening tests\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would require a group of postpartum experts to conduct a\ncomprehensive study and prepare a report on the differential impacts of\npostpartum depression screening measures in relation to black and brown\nwomen and birthing people.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one - Explains who will help conduct the study, that a report\nmust be made, what the study must be on, and the goal of the study which\nis to minimize disparities found in the current postpartum mood screen-\ning tools. The bill identifies what the study will focus on including\nidentifying racial disparities, reviewing current protocols, and evalu-\nating how those protocols can change current inadequate screenings.\nLastly, The bill identifies some but not all possible topics that could\nbe included within new screening measures.\n\nSection two - sets forth the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill directs the Office of Mental Health and the Department of\nHealth to detect racial and other disparities within postpartum\ndepression screenings, and research and analyze new ways to better\ndetect postpartum depression. A focus will be placed on black and brown\nwomen specifically given the disparate impact of postpartum depression\non these women and families.\n\nAccording to both the Center for Disease Control (CDC)and the National\nAlliance on Mental Illness (NAMI) 1 in 8 women suffer from maternal\ndepression. NAMI states that black women are three times more likely to\nexperience maternal depression. It is estimated that over half of the\ninstances of maternal depression in women of color go unreported.\n\nThe current depression screening test is the Edinburgh Postnatal\nDepression Scale (EPDS). Experts have stated, and community leaders have\ntold us, that this test was designed with higher and middle-income white\nwomen in mind. As such, screening often does not take social determi-\nnants of health into account-food scarcity, systemic racism, housing\ninsecurity and more-and it is less effective at detecting maternal\ndepression in black and brown women, as well as in nonbinary and trans-\nmasculine gestational parents.\n\nPostpartum depression can have significant negative consequences, not\nonly for those affected but for their children and families. In asking\nprofessionals to reevaluate current postpartum depression screenings and\nfactor in modern medical understandings and social determinants of\nhealth, we may facilitate the creation of new screening tools able to\n\nbetter diagnose and treat the tens of thousands of mothers and families\naffected by postpartum depression annually.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nTBD.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
